Thanks! This code works. But it still remains a problem for me. What if I need to arrange two columns with different widths in a row, for example: 
```python
col1, col2 = st.columns([2, 1])
```

> [USER] can you share with us the way please ? :)

for example, when I need to arrange two columns with 

```python
col1, col2 = st.columns([5, 1])
```

I write css style like follows:

```
[data-testid="column"] {
    width: calc(25% - 1rem) !important;
    flex: 1 1 calc(25% - 1rem) !important;
    min-width: calc(20% - 1rem) !important;
}
.css-1l269bu {max-width:20% !important;}
```

where 'css-1l269bu' is the auto-generated class for the "col2" in Python code.

it's not elegant but just works haha~

That worked for me 👌
```
st.write('''
    <style>
    [data-testid="column"] {
        flex: 0 0 auto !important; /* Prevent columns from resizing */
        width: calc(100% * var(--col-width)) !important; /* Fixed width based on proportions */
    }
    [data-testid="stHorizontalBlock"] {
        display: flex !important;
        flex-wrap: nowrap !important; /* Prevent wrapping of columns */
        gap: 1rem; /* Spacing between columns */
    }
    body {
        overflow-x: auto !important; /* Allow horizontal scrolling if needed */
    }
    </style>
    ''', unsafe_allow_html=True)
```
